<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    The half of the link that never opens Rhino.

    The other two plugins live inside a running Rhino and report on it. This one starts a Rhino core in its
    own process, headless and without a window, and an agent can work on documents with no splash screen on
    screen. Rhino.Inside does the loading. This assembly adds the setup the loading needs to survive: the
    native opennurbs directory, the resolver, and the rule that no RhinoCommon type may be named in the
    method that starts it.

    The interface matches Phenome.Apps.RhinoLink, adapted to a Rhino that was never opened: plain HTTP on
    127.0.0.1, one JSON response per request, and an ephemeral port in a discovery file. Clients use the same
    discovery and calling conventions, with a smaller verb set because a windowless core cannot run Rhino
    commands.

    Document tooling (explode, rename, normalize, silhouette, info) lives in a repository of its own. It is
    not part of the link: it is specific to one pipeline, and this repository ships publicly to all Rhino
    users.

    That tooling needs a headless core too. It takes HeadlessRhino from here by ProjectReference and carries
    no copy of its own, because the two always travel together (decided on 2026-08-19). HeadlessRhino is
    therefore public surface with a consumer outside this repository: Start, Prepare, SystemDirectory, Invoke,
    Serve, Stop. Renaming any of them, or making the type internal, breaks a build that is not in this
    solution and reports nothing here.

    Referencing an Exe project is deliberate and works: the SDK produces a .dll beside the .exe, and a
    consumer can use HeadlessRhino while this project keeps its own server entry point.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0-windows</TargetFramework>
    <RootNamespace>Phenome.Apps.RhinoInsideLink</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>Phenome.Apps.RhinoInsideLink</AssemblyName>

    <Version>0.1.0</Version>

    <Platforms>x64</Platforms>
    <PlatformTarget>x64</PlatformTarget>

    <IncludeSourceRevisionInInformationalVersion>false</IncludeSourceRevisionInInformationalVersion>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <!--
      NU1701: the Rhino packages are still .NET Framework ones. NU1608: Rhino.Inside pins RhinoCommon to the
      version it shipped against, while a newer one is referenced deliberately. At run time neither loads,
      because the resolver passes both to the Rhino that is actually installed.
    -->
    <NoWarn>NU1701;NU1608</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="RhinoCommon" Version="8.21.25188.17001" ExcludeAssets="runtime" />
    <PackageReference Include="Rhino.Inside" Version="8.0.6-beta" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The same shared source the other two links compile, without Pulse and Picture. Picture draws a view, and a
    windowless core has none. Pulse determines everything from Rhino's main window and its idle events, and a
    windowless core has neither: MainWindowHandle returns zero, and Pulse would report "no dialog" forever. The exclusion is written out explicitly. A dialog can still appear here,
    and it cannot be found this way; Documents.cs suppresses the one that has been observed.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="..\Phenome.Apps.Shared\**\*.cs"
             Exclude="..\Phenome.Apps.Shared\Pulse.cs;..\Phenome.Apps.Shared\Picture.cs"
             LinkBase="Shared" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
